WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.102
Recall at k only checks whether the correct source appears somewhere in the retrieved set.

00:00:07.102 --> 00:00:11.472
Mean Reciprocal Rank, or MRR, also cares about where that source appears.

00:00:11.472 --> 00:00:16.622
If the correct document is rank one, the reciprocal rank is one.

00:00:16.622 --> 00:00:18.987
Rank two gives one half.

00:00:18.987 --> 00:00:21.832
Rank three gives about one third.

00:00:21.832 --> 00:00:26.703
We calculate that value for every evaluation question and take the mean.

00:00:27.524 --> 00:00:35.436
MRR is useful when the ranking order matters, especially when only the first few chunks will be passed to the language

00:00:35.436 --> 00:00:36.006
model.

00:00:36.006 --> 00:00:43.956
Two systems can have the same Recall at five but very different user experiences if one consistently puts the correct

00:00:43.956 --> 00:00:48.096
evidence at rank one and the other puts it at rank five.

00:00:48.096 --> 00:00:53.859
Use Recall and MRR together to get a clearer picture of retrieval quality.
